# AMBE-3000F exchanges observed on the operator's bench, 2026-09-14.
#
# A DVMEGA DVstick 30 passed through ESXi to the production server
# (192.168.1.247), served by stock AMBEserver version 2015-07-11 at 460800
# baud on the by-id path, spoken to over UDP 2460 by cmd/ambe-probe from the
# Fedora development machine.
#
# One record per line: a name, then the packet as hex. Comments start with '#'.
#
# **These are recordings, not readings.** Everything in manual-examples.hex is
# the manufacturer's printed bytes; everything here is what this chip actually
# sent and received. Where the two agree, a claim is settled twice over.
#
# What this capture proved, none of it inferable:
#
#   - Speech in as type 0x02 produces a channel packet out as type 0x01.
#     Patch 0351 swapped those on a hypothesis and 0352 swapped them back on
#     the strength of a contents page. This is the wire.
#   - The length rule holds in the reply direction: channel-reply declares
#     0x000B field bytes and the whole packet is fifteen.
#   - PKT_RATET with index 33 took effect. CFG1 reads 0x00, so all six RATE
#     pins are low and the board boots at whatever Table 116 gives for a rate
#     control word of zero, which is not index 33. The returned frame carries
#     0x48 bits: 72 bits in 20 ms, which is 3600 bps. The rate was set by the
#     packet and the evidence is the frame size rather than the
#     acknowledgement.
#   - A control field is acknowledged by its own identifier followed by 0x00,
#     as Table 43 says.
#   - The configuration response parses. CFG0 0x05 is packet mode over UART
#     with companding disabled, so 16-bit linear samples. CFG2 0xec has
#     PARITY_ENABLE low, so parity is off -- and that pin has an internal
#     pullup, so floating it would read high and parity would be on. Reading
#     zero means the DVMEGA board ties it low deliberately, which is why stock
#     AMBEserver works on it at all.
#
# speech-request is 160 samples of a 1 kHz sine at amplitude 8000, generated
# by cmd/ambe-probe's own sine(). Its data repeats every eight samples, which
# is one cycle at 8 kHz.

reset-request 6100010033

reset-reply 6100010039

prodid-request 6100010030

prodid-reply 61000B0030414D4245333030304600

version-request 6100010031

version-reply 6100310031563132312E453130302E585858582E433131302E473531342E523031342E41303033303630382E433030323032303800

getcfg-request 6100010036

getcfg-reply 61000400360500EC

ratet-request 610002000921

ratet-reply 610002000900

speech-request 

channel-reply 61000B010148954BE6500310B00777
